  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1207 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nThe current surface analysis shows high pressure centered from \nthe eastern Great Lakes into eastern Canada, while a weak cold \nfront moves across northeast Minnesota. Mid-level cloudiness above\n10,000 feet is drifting across northern Wisconsin ahead of an \nassociated shortwave trough. As the cold front settles over the \nregion tonight, upper-level dynamics will outrun the surface \nboundary, causing the front to lose definition over the state. \nPersistent ridging aloft and light boundary layer winds will \nmaintain generally quiet weather across north-central and \nnortheast Wisconsin through the end of the week.\n\nPatchy Dense Fog Late Tonight Into Thursday: As the remnant cold \nfront stalls over Wisconsin tonight, light boundary layer winds \nand clear to partly cloudy skies will foster favorable radiational\ncooling conditions. Boundary layer moisture saturation between \n2 and 9 AM Thursday will promote patchy dense fog development,\nparticularly across far northeast WI west of the Bay. Rapid fog \ndissipation expected by 9 to 10 AM Thursday as morning solar \nheating drives boundary layer mixing. Conditions will remain\nfavorable for early morning ground fog on Friday as well.  \n\nUnseasonably Warm And Dry Through Saturday Afternoon: Deep-layer \natmospheric subsidence associated with the expansive mid-level \nridge will lock in tranquil and dry weather across the forecast \narea through Saturday afternoon. Inland maximum temperatures will \nwarm into the upper 60s to lower 70s, while onshore lake breezes \nhold shoreline communities along Lake Michigan in the upper 50s to lower\n60s. NBM ensemble guidance exhibits high confidence with tight \n10th to 90th percentile temperature spreads of only 2 to 4 degrees\nF for daily highs through Saturday.\n\nIncreased Rain Chances Saturday Night Through Monday: A northern \nstream shortwave trough advancing across central Canada will push \nan associated cold front into the Upper Midwest Saturday night \ninto Sunday. Isentropic ascent and moisture return along the slow-\nmoving frontal boundary will elevate rain shower chances to 20 to\n40 percent across north-central and northeast Wisconsin from \nSaturday night through Monday. Rainfall probabilities and NBM \n90th percentile liquid accumulations over this time period have\nincreased over the past 24 hours and think rain chances will\nincrease in subsequent forecasts. \n\nSharply Cooler Temperatures Early Next Week: Post-frontal cold air\nadvection will usher in a significantly cooler Canadian airmass\nMonday night through Tuesday, dropping daytime high temperatures\ninto the upper 40s to lower 50s area-wide. High pressure shifting\nsouth toward the region will maintain quiet but crisp fall \nweather through the middle of next week.",
